93606 #edb/ref 1061 41036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193606 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f104bf84-a7fb-5c40-a540-f70c2282aa70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193606 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193607 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193607 #edb/ref 1061 41037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193607 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eb6eb779-446c-51b6-aa71-efb894db7bee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193607 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193608 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193608 #edb/ref 1061 41040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193608 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a4942efc-f12a-525b-912b-a6e1804074c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193608 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193609 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193609 #edb/ref 1061 41043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193609 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c1d7fa6f-6f60-5ecf-a7b2-681a79b62e71" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193609 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193610 #edb/ref 1061 41046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193610 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4242fb45-13fd-57f1-834d-c5d8bf8b6731" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193610 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193719 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193719 #edb/ref 1061 466026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193719 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c89b0360-91da-5424-aedb-da76a815447b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193719 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193720 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193720 #edb/ref 1061 466027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193720 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0da282af-995a-5faf-b0d0-876baec229e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193720 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193721 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193721 #edb/ref 1061 466031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193721 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cde1c192-24a5-5dc6-a1eb-22b02b4431e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193721 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193722 #edb/ref 1061 466034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193722 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "44d32822-3dee-5d1f-bc85-937a6b36e438" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193722 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193826 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to compute inverses of reciprocal functions.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the function $f(x)$, given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{7}{2x+4}\n$$\n\nTo find the inverse $f^{-1}(x)$, we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{7}{2x+4}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{7}{2y+4}\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{7}{2y + 4} \\\\\nx(2y + 4) &= 7 \\\\\n2xy + 4x &= 7 \\\\\n2xy &= 7 - 4x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{7 - 4x}{2x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{7 - 4x}{2x}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193826 #edb/ref 1065 6512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193826 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1be3fc12-b770-5a76-ab56-1385a320d85f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193826 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193861 #edb/ref 1064 "When drawing angles expressed in radians, it can be helpful to convert the angle to degrees first. This is especially helpful when writing trigonometric ratios in terms of reference angles, as we usually need to determine which quadrant the angle lies in.\n\nLet's express the following trigonometric ratio in terms of a reference angle:\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nYou might find it tricky to visualize this angle straight off the bat. So, let's start by converting the angle measure into degrees.\n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = 120^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $120^\\circ$, which lies in the $2$nd quadrant.\n\n![](images/34/342602449228f93042121b22f39d6cc203a6d945934a9ddb726e862838832815.png)\n\nNow, to express\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nin terms of $\\sin\\theta_R$, we follow three steps:\n\n1. Find its reference angle $\\theta_R$.\n2. Calculate the value of the function for $\\theta_R$.\n3. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nis in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle $\\theta_R$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ_{R} &= π - θ \\\\\n&= π - \\frac{2π}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3π}{3} - \\frac{2π}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\n, and therefore we're interested in\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta_R = \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nmust be *positive* because the sine ratio is *always* positive in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193861 #edb/ref 1065 6765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193861 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4923e69b-5a86-59ae-ab93-5cd5e68e7ef0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193861 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198141 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13072" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198141 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/95/95c65250736fdfe5989873c31623af8b493ead007a8a799f535111533e899d99.png)\n\nThe Venn diagram shown above represents two events $A$ and $B$ and the number of outcomes in each region of the sample space.\n\nGiven that every outcome is equally likely, find $P\\left(A'\\cap B\\right)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198141 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "225ed6ce-5b8f-5570-85fe-f093c8c7632c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198141 #edb/ref 1074 "To calculate the probability of an event using a Venn diagram, we count the number of outcomes in the region that represents the event, and divide by the total number of outcomes of the sample space $\\mathcal S$.\n\nIn the Venn diagram, $A'\\cap B$ is represented by the area inside the circle $B$ without the intersection between the circles (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/b5/b576c638a88befbc04daf402922f6420fe672fbcc8ca4953a6e391b5b7fa2bb8.png)\n\nWe count up the outcomes:\n\n- The number of outcomes in $A'\\cap B$ is $7$.\n\n- The total number of outcomes in $\\mathcal S$ is\n$9+3+7+11=30$.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP\\left(A'\\cap B\\right)=\\dfrac{7}{30}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198142 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13073" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198142 #edb/ref 1071 "At an audition for the school band, there are $35$ volunteer students in total. Of the volunteers, $20$ students volunteer to play percussion, $15$ students volunteer to play a wind instrument, and $3$ students volunteer for both percussion and wind instruments.\n\nRepresent the corresponding sample space using a Venn diagram. If we randomly select a student among the $35$ students, what is the probability that they volunteered for neither percussion nor wind instruments? Give your answers to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198142 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c613ab13-9671-528c-9e10-1f05ea0b0878" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198142 #edb/ref 1074 "Let us call $A$ the event \"The student volunteers to play percussion\" and $B$ the event \"The student volunteers to play a wind instrument.\"\n\nNote that there are $3$ students who volunteer for both percussion and wind instruments. We draw two overlapping circles representing the events $A$ and $B$ and write the number $3$ where the circles overlap.\n\n![](images/cc/ccc9e4a121d5c3fdd5afc204fcf973ec0c883a53a220846a30b267e0c5e827c1.png)\n\n- The number of students who volunteer for percussion *only* is\n$20 - 3 = 17$,\nso we write the number $17$ in the part of circle $A$ that does not overlap with circle $B$.\n\n![](images/61/61cfe75b341ac014be0f81eb4e58ac605f7680c2f9fc0e08b443e6bd2c9cecb5.png)\n\n- The number of students who volunteer for wind instruments *only* is\n$15 - 3 = 12$,\nso we write the number $12$ in the part of the circle $B$ that does not overlap with circle $A$.\n\n![](images/b7/b704fa8f8e5f77e6188ccb8bbadbb2f3bf6ed8034897b8b1d205574da35030a5.png)\n\nThe total number of students who volunteered for percussion or wind instruments is\n\n$$\n3 + 17 + 12 = 32\n$$\n\nand there are $35$ students in total, so the number of remaining students is\n\n$$\n35 - 32 = 3\n$$\n\nTo represent these $3$ students who did not volunteer for wind instruments or percussion, we write the number $3$ outside of the circles $A$ and $B$.\n\nTherefore, our final Venn diagram looks as shown below:\n\n![](images/1b/1b87d550b2adf6399f31a3c2e8ded4496a02058e904c8682c9c50d26f6ec6d50.png)\n\nThe shaded part of the Venn diagram corresponds to students who volunteered neither for percussion nor wind instruments. The probability that a randomly selected student is from this group is given by\n\n$$\nP(A'\\cap B')=\\dfrac{3}{35} \\approx 0.09\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198144 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13076" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198144 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A$ and $B$ be events. Sketch a Venn diagram that shows a shaded region representing the event $A' \\cup B'$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198144 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8df05d2a-b78f-5515-90e6-27c0264042db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198144 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's first consider the shaded region representing $A'$ and $B'$ separately.\n\n- The event $A'$ is represented by the shaded region below.\n\n![](images/d0/d0b8d298adbec56cf806ddac183e1865bc2908d0fbf75b1935030d491d839850.png)\n\n- The event $B'$ is represented by the shaded region below.\n\n![](images/c7/c7b8767c53739f3f44cc99eee7d1cc24621ab346247c083e9624f8a10fc512e2.png)\n\nFinally, $A' \\cup B'$ is the union of the two shaded regions shown above:\n\n![](images/6c/6cd7efdce23e23882ba6ece09c6f41c67c3678846608491a7ed83bc17c103bf2.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198216 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13998" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198216 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following system of equations.\n${3a + 2b =-2; 4a - 3b = 3$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $a$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $a$, multiply the first equation by $44$\nthe second by $44$ and add the equations." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198216 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b618dc4c-996c-501c-972c-1bcfdeee32be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198216 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the coefficient of $a$ in the first equation is ${\\color{red}{3}}$, while in the second equation it is ${\\color{blue}{4}}$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}{4}}$ and the second equation by $-{\\color{red}{3}}$, then the $a$-coefficient in the first and second equations will become $12$ and $-12$, and these will cancel when we add the equations.\n\n$$\n{(3a + 2b =-2) \\cdot (4); (4a - 3b = 3) \\cdot (-3)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $a$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12a + 8b &= -8 \\\\\n- 12a + 9b &= -9 \\\\\n(8b + 9b) &= (-8 - 9) \\\\\n17b &= -17\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $a$, multiply the first equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}4}$, the second by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-3}$, and add the equations.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198217 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14001" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198217 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following system of equations.\n${4p + 5q = 7; 3p - 2q =-12$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $q$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $q$, multiply the first equation by $44$\nthe second by $44$ and add the equations." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198217 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "68da240e-2c79-50d7-86dd-d203d01dcb32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198217 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the coefficient of $q$ in the first equation is ${\\color{red}{5}}$, while in the second equation it is $-{\\color{blue}{2}}$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ and the second equation by ${\\color{red}{5}}$, then the $q$-coefficient in the first and second equations will become $10$ and $-10$, and these will cancel when we add the equations.\n\n$$\n{(4p + 5q = 7) \\cdot (2); (3p - 2q =-12) \\cdot (5)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $q$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8p + 10q &= 14 \\\\\n15p - 10q &= -60 \\\\\n(8p + 15p) &= (14 - 60) \\\\\n23p &= -46\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $q$, multiply the first equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$, the second by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}5}$, and add the equations.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198218 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14003" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198218 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the solution $(x,y)$ to the following system of equations by eliminating the variable $x{:}$\n\n${2x - 3y =-7; - 3x + 4y = 8$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198218 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c04ac5ff-446a-52d8-b464-88d38efd6088" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198218 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the $x$-coefficient in the first equation is ${\\color{red}2}$, while in the second equation it is $-{\\color{blue}3}$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}3}$ and the second equation by ${\\color{red}2}$, then the $x$-coefficient in the first and second equations will become $6$ and $-6$, and these will cancel when we add the equations.\n\n$$\n{(2x - 3y =-7) \\cdot (3); (-3x + 4y = 8) \\cdot (2)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x - 9y &= -21 \\\\\n- 6x + 8y &= 16 \\\\\n(8y - 9y) &= (16 - 21) \\\\\n- y &= -5 \\\\\ny &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=5$. However, we still need to find $x$. To do this, we substitute $y=5$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - 3y &= -7 \\\\\n2x - 3(5) &= -7 \\\\\n2x - 15 &= -7 \\\\\n2x &= 8 \\\\\nx &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=4$ and $y=5$, which can be written as $(4,5)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198219 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14004" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198219 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the solution $(x,y)$ to the system below by eliminating the variable $y$.\n\n${4x + 3y = 3; 5x + 4y = 5$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198219 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "10d6a803-5114-5995-87c3-1b0e0e87a079" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198219 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is ${\\color{red}3}$, while in the second equation it is ${\\color{blue}4}$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}4}$ and the second equation by $-{\\color{red}3}$, then the $y$-coefficient in the first and second equations will become $-12$ and $12$, and these will cancel when we add the equations.\n\n$$\n{(4x + 3y = 3) \\cdot (4); (5x + 4y = 5) \\cdot (-3)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n16x + 12y &= 12 \\\\\n- 15x - 12y &= -15 \\\\\n(16x - 15x) &= (12 - 15) \\\\\nx &= -3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=-3$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x=-3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x + 3y &= 3 \\\\\n4(-3) + 3y &= 3 \\\\\n- 12 + 3y &= 3 \\\\\n3y &= 15 \\\\\ny &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-3$ and $y=5$, which can be written as $(-3,5)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198350 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198350 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198350 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198350 #edb/ref 1039 "Eliminating the First Variable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198350 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198350 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "772da46a-8b9c-54e7-8661-a5865b1df4e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198351 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198351 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198351 #edb/ref 1039 "Eliminating the Second Variable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198351 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198351 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9cf591e2-45d5-580c-9d46-c6a8645c0513" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198352 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198352 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198352 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198352 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the First Variable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198352 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198352 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e829fbee-ca0a-5cf7-b232-c2c6869cb2a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198353 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198353 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198353 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198353 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198353 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1fc5a393-5fcd-59c9-993b-d900b790e141" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198450 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198450 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198450 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198450 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198450 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Regions on Venn Diagrams Corresponding to Complements" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198450 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198450 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ace27779-e2c4-5bbf-87bc-7e5227a6e5f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198451 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198451 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186199008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198451 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198451 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing a Complement Using a Venn Diagram" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198451 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198451 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "874dfb35-398e-5ed7-8a76-f0011569e6a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198452 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198452 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186199009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198452 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing a Probability Given a Description of a Sample Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198452 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198452 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7cd170a0-47b2-5747-b7a1-13521b841e37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198509 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198509 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198509 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198509 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198509 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198509 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198509 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving Systems of Linear Equations Using Elimination: Two Transformations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198509 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186199235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198509 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198509 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "5e22fbe6-6f4f-523d-81b5-4e8eb335e94e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198529 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198529 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198529 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198529 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198529 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198529 #edb/ref 1045 "Computing Probabilities of Events Containing Complements Using Venn Diagrams" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198529 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186199405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198529 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198529 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "5a962fb0-35f3-53a6-b932-b8fc6eb20898" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199235 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199235 #edb/ref 1061 196706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199235 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3efd94e5-0a3a-5721-9e17-4cc42a121334" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199235 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199236 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199236 #edb/ref 1061 196707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199236 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "88185242-c507-5397-b525-337a9cbf0f57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199236 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199237 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199237 #edb/ref 1061 196710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199237 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d2ed5ea1-20b9-594f-9470-adebce7bf262" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199237 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199238 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199238 #edb/ref 1061 196713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199238 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "067db66a-f3df-588b-a8d5-4ed1d9ae12b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199238 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199239 #edb/ref 1061 196716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199239 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8e9862dd-b298-5880-b133-f1e0adff0ef5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199239 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199405 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199405 #edb/ref 1061 55265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199405 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9fd6356f-9755-518f-977e-b262e57a2132" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199405 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199406 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199406 #edb/ref 1061 55266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199406 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7ef4e24f-93e5-5026-ad4b-dc74096d8a27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199406 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199407 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199407 #edb/ref 1061 55269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199407 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a55c8c4e-50f2-5bfb-8cf8-f98b2797612b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199407 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199408 #edb/ref 1061 55272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199408 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1033361e-36a7-546a-9e14-b22db6eac276" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199408 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199546 #edb/ref 1064 "In a previous lesson, we saw how Venn diagrams can be used to calculate probabilities such as $P(A\\cup B)$ (the union of two events) and $P(A\\cap B)$ (the intersection of two events) for events $A$ and $B$.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll extend our understanding to include event complements.\n\nFor example, consider the event $A \\cap B'$. How might we depict this event on a Venn diagram?\n\nThe idea is to sketch $A$ and $B'$ separately and then find their intersection, as follows:\n\n- The event $A$ is represented by the shaded region below.\n\n![](images/ab/abf925fb7b1e245a27f54851d9065ef1f25281d48a094c5152c586229df0efd4.png)\n\n- The event $B'$ is represented by the shaded region below. It encompasses everything in the sample space $\\mathcal S$ that is **not** in $B$.\n\n![](images/c7/c7b8767c53739f3f44cc99eee7d1cc24621ab346247c083e9624f8a10fc512e2.png)\n\n- Finally, to find $A \\cap B'$, we take the intersection (or common overlap) of the two shaded regions shown above. This gives the following:\n\n![](images/74/74d29171067d70aeadf6eeb520397b0f82b9279ea4981b0f670b0f9a83213a6a.png)\n\nLet's follow a similar process to depict a union of events on a Venn diagram." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199546 #edb/ref 1065 8172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199546 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4bd15252-5112-5483-933b-d799f106d08a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199546 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199598 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{5x + 2y =-1; - 4x - 3y =-2\n$$\n\nSuppose we want to eliminate the variable $x$ from the system using the elimination method.\n\nFirst, notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the *first* equation is ${\\color{red}{5}}$, while in the *second* equation it is $-{\\color{blue}{4}}$.\n\nSo, if we multiply the *first* equation by ${\\color{blue}{4}}$ and the *second* equation by ${\\color{red}{5}}$, then the $x$-coefficient in the first and second equations respectively will become $20$ and $-20$, and these will cancel when we add the equations.\n\n$$\n{(5x + 2y =-1) \\cdot (4); (-4x - 3y =-2) \\cdot (5)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n20x + 8y &= -4 \\\\\n- 20x - 15y &= -10 \\\\\n(8y - 15y) &= (-4 - 10) \\\\\n- 7y &= -14 \\\\\ny &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have successfully eliminated the variable $x$ from the system." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199598 #edb/ref 1065 8705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199598 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e0fe50e3-a9ab-5917-9101-61d27d052fc8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199598 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186210531 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186210532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186210531 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186210533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186210531 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186210534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186210531 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186210535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186210531 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186210536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186210531 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186210531 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186210531 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186210536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186210531 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4fd7528a-eee1-5d01-b7fb-cfa05b48f0f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186210532 #edb/ref 1080 "10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186210532 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186210532 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e9100f91-fc1e-5c98-a41b-4ed3a7acfa72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186210533 #edb/ref 1080 "12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186210533 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186210533 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ed823b62-086b-52e2-9169-be2b4891c218" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186210534 #edb/ref 1080 "24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186210534 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186210534 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f302cf8a-2800-5e54-a999-ea8d52aa8520" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186210535 #edb/ref 1080 "14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186210535 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186210535 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2d33a223-1b57-5f84-a71f-c47693561011" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186210536 #edb/ref 1080 "34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186210536 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186210536 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6a23987b-c061-59bf-85c0-0f6fd3a3635e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212754 #edb/ref 1069 "e-18859" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212754 #edb/ref 1071 "The following equation is true for all values of $x.$\n\n$$\nmx^2 + 5x + 4 = 2x^2 + 5x + 4\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of the constant $m?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212754 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "343c42bc-2a8b-5979-9a21-accb89835444" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212754 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nWe compare the quadratic terms on both sides. On the left-hand side, the coefficient of $x^2$ is $m.$ On the right-hand side, the coefficient of $x^2$ is $2.$\n\nSo, we set\n\n$$\nm = 2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212755 #edb/ref 1069 "e-18860" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212755 #edb/ref 1071 "The equation\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + 12 = 7x^2 + 2x + 12\n$$\n\nis true for all values of $x,$ where $a$ and $b$  are constants. What is the value of $a + b?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212755 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0538ef73-5254-5d99-b709-b08c9ff89c11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212755 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nLet's compare the coefficients:\n\n- The coefficient of $x^2$ on the left-hand side is $a,$ and on the right-hand side it is $7.$ So, we have \n\n$$\na = 7.\n$$\n\n- The coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side is $b,$ and on the right-hand side it is $2.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nb = 2.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =7+2 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212756 #edb/ref 1069 "e-18861" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212756 #edb/ref 1071 "The equation\n\n$$\nx^2 + 9x + 18 = (x + 3)(x + k)\n$$\n\nis true for all values of $x,$ where $k$ is a constant. What is the value of $k?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212756 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "802b17c9-3c7d-58b9-b7ee-8632f4f1cd13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212756 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the factored form on the right-hand side must expand to match the left-hand side.\n\nLet's expand the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)(x+k) & ={x}^{2}+kx+3x+3k \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+(k+3)x+3k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compare the expanded form to the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 9x + 18 = x^2 + (k + 3)x + 3k\n$$\n\nEquating the constant terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18 & =3k \\\\ 6 & =k \\\\ k & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that value $k = 6$ produces the correct linear coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k+3 & =6+3 \\\\  & =9\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich matches the coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side. So, our solution is valid." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212788 #edb/ref 1069 "e-18920" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212788 #edb/ref 1071 "A linear function $g(x)$ gives the remaining mobile data, in gigabytes, on a monthly plan after $x$ days of continuous usage. There are $48$ GB remaining after $2$ days, and $30$ GB remaining after $5$ days. Which equation defines $g(x)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212788 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aaf7aedd-ad76-5b8d-bb6a-3925f8d41006" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212788 #edb/ref 1074 "The linear function modeling this situation is\n\n$$\ng(x) = mx + b\n$$\n\nWe know two points on the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x_{1}, y_{1}) &= (2, 48) \\\\\n(x_{2}, y_{2}) &= (5, 30)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the given two points, we find the slope $m{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{30 - 48}{5 - 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-18}{3} \\\\\n&=-6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the linear function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(x) &= mx + b \\\\\n&=-6x + b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $b$, we substitute one of the points, say $(2, 48)$, into this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n48 &= -6 \\cdot 2 + b \\\\\n48 &= -12 + b \\\\\nb &= 60\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the linear equation that defines $g(x)$ is\n\n$$\ng(x) = -6x + 60\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212789 #edb/ref 1069 "e-18921" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212789 #edb/ref 1071 "A theme park charges $\\[math]65$ per person for the first $30$ people in a group and $\\[/math]50$ for each additional person. Which function $C$ gives the total cost, in dollars, for a group of $g$ people, where $g \\geq 30$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212789 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4fb71375-cea2-5364-b809-c844c462a1a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212789 #edb/ref 1074 "The total cost consists of two parts.\n\n- For the first $30$ people, the park charges\n\n$$\n65 \\times 30 = 1,950\\,\\,\\textrm{dollars}\n$$\n\n- For the additional persons beyond $30$, the park charges\n\n$$\n50(g - 30)\\,\\,\\textrm{dollars}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total cost $C$, in dollars, for a group of $g$ people, where\n\n$$\ng\\geq 30\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC(g) &= 1, 950 + 50(g - 30) \\\\\n&= 1, 950 + 50g - 1, 500 \\\\\n&= 50g + 450\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212793 #edb/ref 1069 "e-18926" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212793 #edb/ref 1071 "| $x$ | $r(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $6$ | $22$ |\n| $9$ | $31$ |\n| $12$ | $40$ |\n\nSome values of the linear function $r$ are shown in the table above. Which of the following defines $r(x)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212793 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "604f7244-01c2-597e-9590-b7ab3965d816" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212793 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $r$ is a linear function, we know it must be of the form\n\n$$\nr(x) = mx + b\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $m$ is the slope, and\n- $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nFirst, we find the slope using two points. For $x = 6$ and $x = 9$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= (r(x_{2}) - r(x_{1}))/(x_{2} - x_{1}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{31 - 22}{9 - 6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{3} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the linear function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(x) &= mx + b \\\\\n&= 3x + b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the $y$-intercept using one point. For $x = 6$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(6) &= 3(6) + b \\\\\n22 &= 18 + b \\\\\nb &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the linear equation that defines $r(x)$ is\n\n$$\nr(x) = 3x + 4\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212794 #edb/ref 1069 "e-18928" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212794 #edb/ref 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac{1}{3-\\sqrt a}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212794 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "659e7fd3-3502-5a47-a54c-3d0c33150d73" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212794 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $3+\\sqrt a$.\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $3+\\sqrt a$, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{3 - \\sqrt{a}} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1}{3 - \\sqrt{a}} \\cdot \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{a}}{3 + \\sqrt{a}} &=  \\\\\n(3 + \\sqrt{a})/((3 - \\sqrt{a})(3 + \\sqrt{a})) &=  \\\\\n(3 + \\sqrt{a})/(3^{2} - (\\sqrt{a})^{2}) &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{a}}{9 - a}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212795 #edb/ref 1069 "e-18929" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212795 #edb/ref 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac {x} {\\sqrt{x+1}+1}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212795 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dfb7701e-d11b-52a0-97fb-8363b1ff0901" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212795 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{x+1}-1$.\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{x+1}-1$, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {x} {\\sqrt{x+1}+1} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {x} {\\sqrt{x+1}+1}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x+1}-1}{\\sqrt{x+1}-1} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {x(\\sqrt{x+1}-1)} {(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)(\\sqrt{x+1}-1)} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {x(\\sqrt{x+1}-1)} {(\\sqrt{x+1})^2-1^2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {x(\\sqrt{x+1}-1)} {x+1-1} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {x(\\sqrt{x+1}-1)} {x} \\\\[5pt] &= \\sqrt{x+1}-1 \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212796 #edb/ref 1069 "e-18930" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212796 #edb/ref 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{x}}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212796 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4bcf9ec5-b5a1-5ab2-bb9f-f22280e072ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186212796 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x}$.\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x}$, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{x}} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{x}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x}} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{x(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x})}{(\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{x})(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{x(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x})}{(\\sqrt{2})^2-(\\sqrt{x})^2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{x(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x})}{2-x} \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213003 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213003 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186213437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213003 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186213438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213003 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213003 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213003 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213003 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213003 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213003 #edb/ref 1039 "Matching a Single Coefficient" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213003 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186212754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213003 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5cf64df7-0abb-5b42-bed6-b3664c2de89a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213004 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186213439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213004 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186213440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213004 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213004 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213004 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213004 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213004 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213004 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213004 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving for Two Coefficients" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213004 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186212755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213004 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b2caeb2c-39cf-595a-8105-67d11bf0e45e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186213005 #edb/